title: 'Kubeconfig exec credential plugin: command and args go through a shell unquoted'
updated: 2026-10-18T06:32:30Z
---

`_exec_credential` (lib/Kubernetes/REST/Kubeconfig.pm:498-518) runs the plugin with backticks: ``my $output = `$cmd @args`;`` (line 510). kubectl/client-go exec the command directly with the args as an argv list. Here the args are joined with spaces and parsed by /bin/sh, so any arg with a space, quote, `$`, `;`, `*`, `&` or `|` is split or interpreted.

Reproduce (kubeconfig user):

```
exec:
  apiVersion: client.authentication.k8s.io/v1
  command: /bin/sh
  args: ["-c", "echo '{\"status\":{\"token\":\"abc\"}}'; echo INJECTED >&2"]
```

`Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig->new(kubeconfig_path => ...)->api` prints `INJECTED` from the *outer* shell and croaks 'exec credential did not return token' - with kubectl the same entry yields the token. Real-world victims: `sh -c` wrappers, `--exec-arg` values with JSON, profile or cluster names with spaces, Windows-style paths. It is also needless shell exposure for a value read from a file.

No test runs an exec plugin with args (t/33 only uses a bare command, t/32 only checks the command is not path-resolved).

Done when: the plugin is started without a shell (`open my $fh, '-|', $cmd, @args` or IPC::Open3), stderr of the plugin stays visible, the exit status is in the croak, and a test covers an arg containing a space and a quote.

